can you just firstly remind our viewers  on why you felt you had to resign from  your post  well mine was a little bit different  from lord guys although lord i think has  since agreed with me with his  resignation letter what i was saying was  that when the prime minister responded  to the sugree report he was urged to  come up with a reply which addressed  whether or not um the survey report said  that he'd broken the ministerial code or  not and he was very very focused on  whether or not he broke the ministerial  code by being issued with a fixed  penalty notice if you remember by the  police didn't really address the  main  criticism in the supreme report which  was the failure of leadership that  sugrade described it as the report said  and of course leadership at number 10 of  the culture the organization that  allowed all those parties to happen  is one of the central seven principles  of integrity and public life they're  called the nolan principles they run  like a sticker rock right the way  through the middle of the ministerial  code and it was pretty hard i thought to  conclude that the prime minister wasn't  being criticized for breaking  ministerial code and when he didn't  really address that i just felt i had to  go but i'm i'm i'm very sorry that it  came to that obviously it wasn't a  decision which i took very lightly and  but that was my reason and i think that  lord guyt actually backed it up as one  of the points he made in his letter uh  when he when he resigned uh earlier on  this week  you say that law guy you felt backed up  that principle but to be honest i think  most people would find it a bit more  easy to get their heads around i guess  if lord gait had resigned for the  reasons that you did and you know he he  didn't go  over the parties at number 10 or over  the flat refurbishment instead it seemed  like he decided to quit after a request  for advice about tariffs on chinese  steel i mean it doesn't really feel like  a smoking gun does it  well and that's been the sort of the big  open question you're absolutely right i  mean everyone's been reading his letter  he's since published a further  clarification but there's an awful lot  of  missing details about precisely what it  was that he was being asked to  to to opine on um and so that's still  sort of an open question which i think  people will still be looking for answers  for because no one can quite understand  why a trade deal might involve a breach  of the ministerial code or not so that's  still an outstanding uh and an open  question which we don't know the answer  to yet  do you think that really you know part  of the reason that he went is because of  a build-up i guess uh over  things like the ministerial code and how  it applies to partygate and receiving  that fine  it was pretty clear from his letter yes  that this was if you like the the final  straw that broke the camel's back um and  that he'd been sort of getting more and  more uncomfortable over time one of the  things that made him uncomfortable as i  said was the reason why i resigned about  a week earlier and but yeah ultimately  we will have to see whether or not he  there's any more clarification he can he  can offer um or indeed whether or not  parliament wants to see what it was that  he was being asked to to opine on um but  we'll have to wait and see about that  one as you say still many questions to  be answered i think now one of the  reasons i was keen to talk to you about  this subject today is because it feels  as if number 10 well they have said that  they are now reviewing  the post of ethics advisor in its  entirety do you think they might scrap  it well i hope they won't i think that  the ethics advisor does something really  important and while it's quite possible  you do need to update and upgrade and  modernize these things every once in a  while it's quite possible to  amend it a bit i think that if they do  anything other than strengthen it then i  think  they will lose a lot of credibility  and they will also  miss out on a job that urgently needs to  be done i think over the last few months  clearly demonstrate that it has to  happen so yes there are a couple of  things they could do to improve it to  strengthen it to modernize it and  scrapping which is not part of that  do you think that part of it might be  driven for the fact that  it might be quite difficult to get  anybody to do the job  because  well i i think that there will be an  issue with finding somebody yes um but i  think that if they strengthen the role  i've made a couple of suggestions about  how that might happen it becomes more  doable if you make it stronger and  therefore it will become i think more  attractive to the kind of  sturdily independent high caliber person  they're going to need to have to give  the role credibility and therefore to  sort of restore the credibility in this  area of the ministerial code and of  number 10. so yes it can be done um but  they'll need to strengthen it in order  to make it attractive to the right kind  of person  the right kind of person being someone  who will stand up to boris johnson is  that what you mean  yes absolutely and not just boris  johnson that the whole point about this  role is that they need to be willing to  stand up to any prime minister um yeah  whoever they may be boris johnson or any  of his successors over the next you know  10 20 30 years you've got to have  somebody who isn't afraid to speak truth  to power now i just want to talk to you  a little bit about the uh confidence uh  motion 148 mps of course voting to say  they had no confidence in the prime  minister we heard from grant schatz  earlier on the program who quite  interestingly said he predicted that  number and was just worn out in his  prediction which is quite uh remarkable  actually uh but he was making the point  that he thinks now most people want  everyone to move on for the prime  minister to focus on doing the job  looking at the situation in ukraine etc  is that analysis correct or do you think  that uh a significant number of  conceptual mps are still  harboring those concerns about his  leadership  well i think that the  by winning the vote even though i'm sure  he would have wanted to win it but much  more handily and with a bigger margin  but by winning the vote i think the  prime minister has won himself a bit of  time um and people like me and others we  have to respect the result of that vote  we have to accept it and respect it and  i think everybody does and that means  that the prime minister's got a little  bit of time to  you know deliver the reset that he's  already said he wants to do he knows  he's got to build some you know rebuild  some bridges if you like so he's he said  he wants to do that i think that's  entirely the right thing for him to do  but i don't think that rebuilding  bridges will just involve ignoring the  issue because i don't think ethics works  that way i don't think that integrity  works the way i think you've got to show  that you're changing what you're doing  and you're changing how you do it in  order to address the concerns you can't  just ignore them and pretend it didn't  happen  on sunday reporting today uh that some  mps are looking at potentially changing  the rules around confidence motions so  that another one could be held after six  months  is that correct  well i think that would be a decision  for  sigron brady who runs the the 1922  committee which is kind of like the the  trade union if you like for for  conservative mps  and i  my guess is that what he would i think  he's actually said this publicly is that  there's not much point in changing the  rules unless it's quite likely that  another vote would come up with a  different result um so i think it would  require quite a lot of uh conservative  mps who voted in favor of the prime  minister in the last vote to come to him  privately and say look i've changed my  mind and only at that point didn't make  very much sense otherwise you're just  sort of going through  sort of party constitutional hoops for  no very good practical purpose and  what's the point of that  but it would be uh possible to do uh if  enough concerto mps indicated that  they'd like to see another one  well at that point there's a process and  and the  votes and stuff like that but yes you  could do it um but i think that everyone  is  playing that one quite carefully and not  wanting sort of fan of the flames of  that idea just because there's not much  point um if if the reset works if the  prime minister persuades people if he  rebuilds the bridges that he said he  wants to do um then it all becomes a bit  sort of a theoretical argument i think  is the point  okay that makes sense uh thank you very  much for being on the program this  morning john penrose there

